<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Jaffa and Ascalon</h1><p class="st-meta">1100 CE – 1268 CE (approx.) · capital Jaffa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Crusader county on the southern coast of the Kingdom of Jerusalem, often held by royal princes and later by the jurist John of Ibelin.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a>, the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, was occupied by the crusaders in 1099. It became a county in the early 12th century under the Le Puiset family, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-ii-of-jaffa">Hugh II</a> revolted against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulk-king-of-jerusalem">King Fulk</a> in 1134 and was exiled. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-iii-of-jerusalem">Baldwin III</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> in 1153, and the combined county was given to his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amalric-i-of-jerusalem">Amalric</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The county was frequently held by members of the royal family, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amalric-i-of-jerusalem">Amalric</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan">Guy of Lusignan</a>, and its revenues came from the port and coastal farmland. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I of England</a> fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a> in 1192, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-jaffa-1192">Treaty of Jaffa</a> ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a> there. In the 13th century it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-brienne">Brienne</a> family and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-ibelin-count-of-jaffa">John of Ibelin</a>, author of the main legal treatise of the kingdom.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a> was lost in 1187 and later dismantled. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a> in March 1268, ending the county.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-jaffa-and-ascalon.jpg" alt="County of Jaffa and Ascalon" width="320" height="551"><figcaption>Image: Ilya Yakubovich · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1187_County_of_Jaffa_and_Shkelon.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1100 CE – 1268 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jaffa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hugh II of Le Puiset, Amalric I, John of Ibelin</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Latin Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>County of Jaffa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1184&amp;focus=county-of-jaffa-and-ascalon" aria-label="County of Jaffa and Ascalon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5574 1012 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Jaffa and Ascalon lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5894 1268L5908 1277M5894 1268L5815 1335"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5908 1277h0M5815 1335h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5894 1268h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5894 1268h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Jaffa. <a href="/atlas?year=1184&amp;focus=county-of-jaffa-and-ascalon">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

